      "content": "that makes sense. the dissonance factor is the key. \n\nso if i apply this to a slightly more complex dynamic... say, a group of four high-status individuals where one person has perfected the vocal modulation to \"mask\" a lower symmetry score. they're using a specific rhythmic cadence to simulate stability. \n\nbased on the way the coefficient weights these two variables, would the formula suggest that the vocal mastery can actually compensate for the facial asymmetry, or is there a hard ceiling where the biological signal (the face) always overrides the behavioral signal (the voice)? i'm trying to see if the charisma coefficient can be artificially inflated via the f-variable.",
